                        "entry_id": "dcf5f6f1-24fb-35ec-9128-0f1621a8830f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of or pertaining to the genus Pavo or its family Pavonidae, including the peafowl."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5f7eaa36-cb3d-3b83-bc6d-342cf42756bc",
                        "entry_id": "dcf5f6f1-24fb-35ec-9128-0f1621a8830f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Possessing the coloring or iridescence of a peacock feather."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "77c7fea1-64c7-4cdd-81b5-d2ef6cc64ef9",
                                "sense_id": "5f7eaa36-cb3d-3b83-bc6d-342cf42756bc",
                                "text": "These rocks are a schist, and abound with the copper pyrites, which occasionally exhibits a pavonine tarnish. The schist is sometimes of a very deep gray colour, and is then the most rich in metal; sometimes also it has the green[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5055a49f-672e-4dae-b16e-053067e7ad2d",
                                "sense_id": "5f7eaa36-cb3d-3b83-bc6d-342cf42756bc",
                                "text": "It resembled from a distance porphyry, while much of it had a pavonine lustre, like the argentiferous galena of the Silver States in North America. The great weight suggested one mass of metal, and part of it had evidently been[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c415524f-c22f-452b-a73e-ec931d92bdac",
                                "sense_id": "5f7eaa36-cb3d-3b83-bc6d-342cf42756bc",
                                "text": "362. Massive BORNITE, having a pavonine purple tarnish; with dolomite. Kenmare mine, Co. Kerry, Ireland. 363. Massive BORNITE, having a pavonine purple tarnish; with malachite and ochre."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "44ec207e-7378-4b61-ac2a-2dd609e71102",
                                "sense_id": "5f7eaa36-cb3d-3b83-bc6d-342cf42756bc",
                                "text": "In D. kahli the blue of the wings on the upper side is deep pavonine blue, or intense cobalt, confined on the anterior wing to a broad patch above the lower margin near the lower angle, and not invading the cell;[…]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "173c85ab-df28-33f9-884a-94c12cd45ba2",
                        "entry_id": "dcf5f6f1-24fb-35ec-9128-0f1621a8830f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Showy, like a peacock's tail; exhibiting vanity."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2ac40325-b2f9-4400-9b83-d37f55cf6a95",
                                "sense_id": "173c85ab-df28-33f9-884a-94c12cd45ba2",
                                "text": "... leaving its present body would find or construct another according to its chief intrinsic qualities and forces, whether those were a leonine magnanimity of courage, a vulpine subtlety of cunning, or a pavonine strut of vanity ."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b88cb506-4ff7-4057-af64-3304a335ef5d",
                                "sense_id": "173c85ab-df28-33f9-884a-94c12cd45ba2",
                                "text": "He had me all to himself; whilst his wife abased herself before my companion, he made up for the abasement by a truly pavonine strut and spread of his feathers. Amongst the few items of fact that floated on the torrent of his [lecture were...]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e141a5ae-18b6-469a-b425-273fd6cfd06d",
                                "sense_id": "173c85ab-df28-33f9-884a-94c12cd45ba2",
                                "text": "A list of acknowledgements can be a pavonine affair, an excuse more to flaunt than to thank. I have tried to avoid that here. But I must thank my excellent colleagues at QMUL,[…]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
